insert into public.questions (id, test_id, prompt, options, position, passage) values
  ('11d3e8a2-0c94-531b-8664-cdab15e800a3', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["reputation for", "requirement for", "complaint about", "question about"]'::jsonb, 1, 'British painter Peter Edwards has a _____ painting portraits of notable figures from a variety of different fields. These characteristic works include his esteemed portraits of poet Seamus Heaney and casting director Ruth Lambert.'),
  ('39397538-fa50-51ad-bdb5-5eb3f62ead87', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["guessed", "blamed", "received", "forgot"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Ji-li Jiang’s book Red Kite and Blue Kite has earned a lot of praise. In fact, the book _____ the Asian/Pacific American Award for Literature.'),
  ('a505b053-733f-5f52-adac-422d9bbefdd8', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["complicated", "representative", "available", "predictable"]'::jsonb, 3, 'The equipment from the Apollo Moon landings (1969-1972), such as solar wind sensors and seismic sensors, remains there to this day, but the data from these missions were mostly inaccessible until a recent data transfer project made them _____ . This project has allowed researcher Renee Weber to make use of the information in investigating tectonic activity on the Moon.'),
  ('03180e92-c554-5ba1-8a0c-b50cd8049ec7', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?', '["assess", "utilize", "influence", "undermine"]'::jsonb, 4, 'One way to _____ the importance of a scholar’s research is to track how often other scholars refer to that research. For example, University of California-Berkeley economist Ulrike Malmendier, who studies behavioral economics, is among the world’s most frequently cited researchers in her field, indicating that her work has been quite significant.'),
  ('93f32211-f90e-5aac-85fa-6b10ac243f9b', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice best describes the overall structure of the text?', '["It provides a history of research into a biological process and suggests a direction for further investigation.", "It explains why an organism poses a threat and then suggests how that threat might be avoided.", "It depicts two organisms and presents their similarities and differences.", "It describes a biological process and explains why researching it is important."]'::jsonb, 5, 'Horizontal gene transfer involves the exchange of genetic material between organisms not in a parent-offspring relationship. It is common among prokaryotes (single-celled organisms, such as the bacteria Carnobacterium viridans and Lactococcus lactis). The process can have the effect of increasing bacterial resistance to antibiotics; as such, an understanding of how to prevent horizontal gene transfer might result in the mitigation of dangerous pathogens (organisms that cause disease).'),
  ('2ec2d908-5728-5496-a396-d7a8d010e12f', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Based on the texts, how would the author of Text 2 most likely respond to the underlined claim in Text 1?', '["By arguing that UNESCO’s recognition of Bergamo was well deserved because of the city’s delicious food.", "By suggesting that Bergamo’s status as a City of Gastronomy may not be well known outside of Italy.", "By noting that there are other benefits to being named a City of Gastronomy besides increased tourism.", "By claiming that Bergamo’s chefs were mainly responsible for the city’s recognition by UNESCO."]'::jsonb, 6, '**Text 1**

In 2019 Bergamo, Italy, was named a City of Gastronomy by UNESCO in recognition of the city’s unique food culture. <u>The honor is well known among both residents of the city and tourists who visit.</u> It’s obvious that the selection of Bergamo by UNESCO has brought awareness to local recipes, cooking practices, and chefs and has provided a boost to the city’s tourism industry.

**Text 2**

Many residents of Bergamo hoped that food tourism would increase after the city was chosen as a City of Gastronomy in 2019. However, as researcher Eerang Park and colleagues argue, cities must still create effective marketing strategies to benefit fully from being named a City of Gastronomy. Without an intentional effort to promote the city’s food scene, many current and potential visitors to Bergamo may not even be aware that it’s home to uniquely delicious food.'),
  ('de424ce7-fdc3-5538-9b18-1a73aa1d9f19', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'According to the text, what is true about the aria “I Got Plenty of Nuthin''”?', '["It appears in the Gershwin opera Porgy and Bess but not in the adaptation by Davis and Evans.", "A brief quotation from it appears in Davis and Evans’s adaptation of Porgy and Bess.", "Listeners familiar with Gershwin’s body of work generally disliked it.", "It was adapted by Davis in a recording that appears on his album Milestones."]'::jsonb, 7, 'Known for the albums Quiet Nights and Milestones, jazz trumpeter Miles Davis collaborated several times with pianist Gil Evans. Their 1958 adaptation of George Gershwin’s opera Porgy and Bess bears little resemblance to the 1935 original. Davis and Evans felt no desire to please listeners expecting an exact duplication of the opera. They omitted parts, such as the aria “I Got Plenty of Nuthin'',” and sometimes made only brief gestures toward Gershwin’s melodies. But Davis and Evans’s willingness to recompose Gershwin’s work led to one of the most enduring albums in Davis’s catalog.'),
  ('89d72ff4-0377-55f0-a28b-dbef2cbf2a58', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '["Words committed to print have a greater permanence than messages that are merely spoken aloud.", "People are less likely to forget a message when they hear it spoken aloud than they are when they read it in print.", "Unless a spoken message is delivered by an expert, it can be safely ignored.", "Most authors have little hope of being remembered well past their lifetimes."]'::jsonb, 8, 'The following text is adapted from Daniel Defoe’s 1704 nonfiction book The Storm.

The sermon is a sound of words spoken to the ear, and prepared only for present meditation, and extends no farther than the strength of memory can convey it; a book printed is a record, remaining in every man’s possession, always ready to renew its acquaintance with his memory, and always ready to be produced as an authority or voucher to any reports he makes out of it, and conveys its contents for ages to come, to the eternity of mortal time, when the author is forgotten in his grave.'),
  ('f77d67ed-179c-590a-ba23-d4fd42697e31', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the statement?', '["female that was 13 years old.", "female that was 6 years old.", "male that was 10 years old.", "male that was 3 years old."]'::jsonb, 9, '**Brown Bears in Katmai National Park, Alaska**

| Bear Identification number | Sex | Age(years) | Approximate weight number(pounds) |
| 106 | female | 6 | 400 |
| 119 | male | 10 | 800 |
| 183 | female | 13 | 375 |
| 122 | male | 3 | 200 |

Scientists collected information about brown bears in Katmai National Park in Alaska. This information included each bear’s sex, age, and approximate weight. The bear with the lowest approximate weight shown in the table was a _____'),
  ('e9e34ac3-873e-5999-8d9a-e0441717a756', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to complete the assertion?', '["90 streaky rockskippers.", "610 streaky rockskippers.", "125 streaky rockskippers.", "249 streaky rockskippers."]'::jsonb, 10, '**Cumulative Counts of Fish in Three Taiwanese Tide Pools, 1999-2018**

| Species | Station 1 | Station 2 | Station 3 |
| barred flagtail | 249 | 64 | 16 |
| streaky rockskipper | 125 | 139 | 610 |
| blackspotted rockskipper | 83 | 74 | 31 |
| Cocos frillgoby | 50 | 64 | 90 |

Lin-Tai Ho and colleagues tracked fish populations in three tide pool–monitoring stations in Taiwan from 1999 to 2018. The streaky rockskipper was especially common at station 3: there, the researchers counted _____'),
  ('b668583e-a16d-533f-a206-eb4ee01564e6', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which quotation from Cane most effectively illustrates the claim?', '["\"And when the wind is from the South, soil of my homeland falls like a fertile shower upon the lean streets of [Washington, DC].\"", "\"The young trees had not outgrown their [planter] boxes then. V Street [in Washington, DC] was lined with them.\"", "“The Capitol dome looked like a gray ghost ship drifting in from sea.”", "“The [train] engines of this valley have a whistle, the echoes of which sound like iterated gasps and sobs. I always think of them as crude music.”"]'::jsonb, 11, 'Cane is a 1923 novel by Jean Toomer. In one portion of the novel, Toomer uses figurative language to connect the narrator’s urban environment of Washington, DC, and the rural South of the narrator’s past, writing, _____'),
  ('23cf0891-139d-5b97-be1e-47a8da0bf644', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which quotation from a study of irrigation best supports the student’s claim?', '["“The first known irrigation system was built in the 7th century CE by the Hohokam people, a Native population in the American Southwest that inhabited central Arizona.”", "“Roughly 70% of the world’s water withdrawals, from sources such as rivers, lakes, and aquifers, is for irrigation water used to meet various agricultural demands.”", "“Today, many areas of the southwestern United States utilize sprinkler irrigation systems, which require machinery that can spray water in all directions.”", "“The irrigation system developed by the Hohokam people in the 7th century CE in what is now known as central Arizona was simple but applied hydraulic engineering design features that are still used today.”"]'::jsonb, 12, 'A student is writing a research paper on the history of irrigation in the southwestern United States, situating the development of Lake Sabrina (a man-made body of water created in Inyo County, California, in 1908) in a larger historical context. In order to emphasize irrigation’s importance, the student claims that irrigation is humanity’s principal use of water.'),
  ('32ee32bc-902b-5d41-b71f-62a44cbe2e92', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which finding, if true, would best support the researcher’s hypothesis?', '["Languages that emerged in northwestern North America tend to have more sounds than languages that emerged in East Asia do, even though northwestern North America is farther away from Africa than East Asia is.", "Languages that emerged in Eastern Europe tend to have more sounds than languages that emerged in East Asia do, and Eastern Europe is closer to Africa than East Asia is.", "Languages that emerged in Western Europe tend to have fewer sounds than languages that emerged in East Asia do, and Western Europe is closer to Africa than East Asia is.", "Languages that emerged in Southeast Asia tend to have more sounds than languages that emerged in East Asia do, even though the two regions are roughly the same distance from Africa."]'::jsonb, 13, 'The Arapaho language of the Plains region in the United States has 16 vowel and consonant sounds. In contrast, the Hadza language of eastern Africa has 70. Why would languages differ in this way? One researcher has hypothesized that when modern humans arose in Africa, they spoke a single language, but as humans gradually spread throughout Africa and then around the globe, that language developed into new languages. Those developed into still more languages as small bands of humans spread even farther, with each new language retaining fewer sounds from humanity’s original language.'),
  ('6109ad94-5b09-55cf-9c09-3e463fc4d960', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["identify molecular components similar to EBF that target the activation of Or31 receptors.", "investigate alternative methods for extracting EBF molecules from chrysanthemums.", "verify the precise locations of Or31 and other odor receptors on mosquitoes'' antennae.", "determine the maximum number of different odor receptors that can be activated by a single molecule."]'::jsonb, 14, 'Many mosquito repellents contain natural components that work by activating multiple odor receptors on mosquitoes'' antennae. As the insects develop resistance, new repellents are needed. Ke Dong and her team found that EBF, a molecular component of a chrysanthemum-flower extract, can repel mosquitoes by activating just one odor receptor—and this receptor, Or31, is present in all mosquito species known to carry diseases. Therefore, the researchers suggest that in developing new repellents, it would be most useful to _____ .'),
  ('80e7ffee-de46-5f86-a214-098e0315e385', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most logically completes the text?', '["many young people think they can volunteer only within their own communities.", "volunteering may be even more helpful than many young people think it is.", "volunteering can help society overall more than it can help individual people.", "many young people may not know how to find ways to volunteer their time."]'::jsonb, 15, 'Volunteering, or giving time for a community service for free, is a valuable form of civic engagement because helping in a community is also good for society as a whole. In a survey of youths in the United States, most young people said that they believe volunteering is a way to help people on an individual level. Meanwhile, only 6% of the youths said that they think volunteering is a way to fix problems in society overall. These replies suggest that _____'),
  ('d9af71ff-dafe-5562-adcd-03e82ab856fc', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["has he become such a celebrated talent?", "has he become such a celebrated talent.", "he has become such a celebrated talent?", "he has become such a celebrated talent."]'::jsonb, 16, 'In 2014, actor Keith David voiced the character Old Buzzard in the film Birds of Paradise. David has acted in everything from commercials to Broadway musicals, but his voice acting work is why _____'),
  ('5093beb3-8e8d-5b13-9fff-b30cf78e9484', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["was", "have been", "are", "were"]'::jsonb, 17, 'Ann Greely of Maine _____ a fierce advocate of women’s voting rights in the late 1800s. The dedication of Greely and her fellow activists was rewarded in 1920, when the Nineteenth Amendment to the US Constitution guaranteed American women the right to vote.'),
  ('0fdeb694-6427-5d01-9d1d-9460402c81dc', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["is", "have been", "are", "are being"]'::jsonb, 18, 'The city of Quito in Ecuador _____ one of the rare places in the world located almost directly on the equator.'),
  ('0897730d-8583-58dd-9342-7c2ed2cb4eeb', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["from,18.4°C at", "from,18.4°C at,", "from 18.4°C at", "from:18.4°C at,"]'::jsonb, 19, 'On February 1, 2018, Florida-based researchers Martha A. Scholl, Maoya Bassiouni, and Angel J. Torres-Sánchez compiled climate data from several sites in Puerto Rico’s Luquillo Mountains. At CC1, the site with the highest elevation, the air temperature fluctuated slightly throughout the day, _____ 11:00 a.m. to 16.2°C at 7:00 p.m.'),
  ('13d5e649-5059-5521-b154-454453943cc1', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["occurred", "occurring", "occur", "will occur"]'::jsonb, 20, 'To study the ultrafast movements of atoms, chemist Ahmed Zewail had to devise a novel spectroscopy technique capable of measuring chemical reactions _____ within molecules at a quadrillionth of a second. Ultimately, Zewail succeeded and was eventually honored with the Robert A. Welch Award and the Albert Einstein World Award of Science for this groundbreaking achievement.'),
  ('f275cac5-954b-53e9-8e66-3c2385e324da', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?', '["path, which is about 0.38 miles long", "path which is about 0.38 miles long", "path which is about 0.38 miles long,", "path, which is about 0.38 miles long,"]'::jsonb, 21, 'Many experts, like lawyer and cycling advocate Ernesto Hernandez-Lopez, have proposed bike travel as one possible way to alleviate congestion on the busy roadways of Los Angeles County, California. Indeed, local bicycle paths like the Harbor Park bicycle _____ have become an increasingly popular means of travel for commuter and recreational trips alike.'),
  ('f675c41a-108c-5f7d-a269-727f932620bb', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Finally,", "However,", "Therefore,", "Actually,"]'::jsonb, 22, 'When using hairy coneflower for dyeing cloth, textile artists first soak it in water to release its color. Next, they add the cloth to the dye bath and simmer it for hours, perhaps even days. _____ they remove the cloth from the dyebath, revealing that the cloth has turned a vibrant blue color.'),
  ('2e96d9db-10e1-57ba-807f-9072b0a05647', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?', '["Besides,", "However,", "On the contrary,", "In fact,"]'::jsonb, 23, 'Though Middle English was widely spoken in fourteenth-century England, the English language was rarely employed in literature until poet Geoffrey Chaucer pioneered its literary use. _____ his manuscripts contain the first documented uses of over 2,000 English words—like the word “conspiracy” in his 1386 poem “The Monk’s Tale”—which led a contemporary to dub him “the first finder of our fair language.”'),
  ('85603b04-4cd6-5b27-bfc3-6fa2f5f51822', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to emphasize why Stradivarius violins are prized by musicians?', '["Many Stradivarius violins, like the Leonora Jackson, get their name from a former owner.", "Among musicians, violins made by Antonio Stradivari are renowned for their sound quality.", "Antonio Stradivari, a famous violin maker, lived from 1644 to 1737.", "Leonora Jackson was an American violinist who once owned a Stradivarius violin."]'::jsonb, 24, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Antonio Stradivari (1644–1737) made about 1,000 violins in his lifetime.
Musicians prize his Stradivarius violins for their famed sound quality.
Many of the 500 or so that exist today are named for a previous owner.
The Leonora Jackson Stradivarius is named for Leonora Jackson, an American violinist.'),
  ('ccbc5696-70a3-542e-9d05-6967ec80244b', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to specify Bellini’s role in neutrino research?', '["Particle physicist Gianpaolo Bellini has worked to advance the scientific understanding of neutrinos through his research on solar and geoneutrinos.", "Neutrinos are a type of subatomic particle that particle physicists are still trying to understand.", "First discovered in the mid-twentieth century, neutrinos are a type of subatomic particle.", "Gianpaolo Bellini is recognized for his particle physics research."]'::jsonb, 25, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Gianpaolo Bellini is an Italian particle physicist.
Particle physicists study subatomic particles.
Neutrinos are some of the least understood subatomic particles.
Neutrinos were first discovered in the mid-twentieth century.
Bellini is known for his research on solar and geoneutrinos.'),
  ('f22517de-3bcf-5671-8fe3-099114d9804d', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two artifacts. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?', '["The buffalo horn spoon (ivpish ishtimpa in Choctaw) is featured in the Hina Hanta archive.", "Featured in the Hina Hanta archive, the buffalo horn spoon and the beaded leather purse are both cultural artifacts relevant to the history of the Choctaw people.", "Not all artifacts in the Hina Hanta archive are from the 1800s; indeed, the beaded leather purse is from 1949.", "There are more recent artifacts in the Hina Hanta archive than the buffalo horn spoon from 1800."]'::jsonb, 26, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

Hina Hanta is an online archive curated by the Choctaw Nation of Oklahoma.
It features images of cultural artifacts relevant to the history of the Choctaw people. It features a spoon (ivpish ishtimpa in Choctaw) made of buffalo horn.
The spoon was made in 1800.
Hina Hanta features a purse (shukcha in Choctaw) made of beaded leather.
The purse was made in 1949.'),
  ('353d5449-ae7e-590a-b018-63d71de85f23', '4e37d0e8-098f-5d70-8240-a5c9f73254d3', 'Which choice most effectively uses information from the given sentences to explain the role of color consultants at Fashion Week?', '["A shade of imaginative purple was an on-trend addition to one color consultant’s report on 2018 London Fashion Week.", "A color consultant report on 2019 New York Fashion Week highlighted the popularity of a deep red shade.", "At Fashion Weeks in 2018 and 2019, shades favored by designers included an imaginative purple and a deep red.", "Each Fashion Week, color consultants compile reports on the shades that have proved popular with fashion designers."]'::jsonb, 27, 'While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

At London and New York Fashion Weeks, fashion designers debut new clothing collections. Color consultants create trend reports on the popular colors at a given Fashion Week. A report on 2018 London Fashion Week noted the popularity of an imaginative purple shade that was cool in tone and bright in intensity.
A report on 2019 New York Fashion Week noted the popularity of a deep red shade that was warm in tone and rich in intensity.')
on conflict (id) do update set test_id = excluded.test_id, prompt = excluded.prompt, options = excluded.options,
  position = excluded.position, passage = excluded.passage;
insert into public.question_keys (question_id, correct_index, correct_answers, explanation) values
  ('11d3e8a2-0c94-531b-8664-cdab15e800a3', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The phrase "reputation for painting portraits" is an idiomatic and precise way to say that Peter Edwards is known for creating portraits; it fits both grammatically and logically with the following gerund phrase "painting portraits of notable figures." The sentence then continues naturally to list examples of those portraits.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Requirement for painting portraits" would mean someone must paint portraits, which doesn’t make sense in context about an artist’s characteristic works.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Complaint about painting portraits" implies criticism, which conflicts with the positive examples that follow (esteemed portraits).
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Question about painting portraits" would suggest uncertainty or inquiry, not the established fact the sentence conveys.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence; the only one that yields a natural, idiomatic meaning is "reputation for."'),
  ('39397538-fa50-51ad-bdb5-5eb3f62ead87', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The verb "received" appropriately and precisely completes the sentence because books (and authors) commonly "receive" awards; the phrase "received the Asian/Pacific American Award for Literature" clearly expresses that the book was given that honor.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Guessed" does not make sense in this context because an award is not something a book can guess, and the sentence requires a verb indicating the book was given the award.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Blamed" is incorrect because it indicates fault or responsibility, which is unrelated to winning or being given an award.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Forgot" is incorrect because it indicates a failure to remember, which is not logically connected to an award being associated with the book.
**Faster approach:** Use collocation knowledge—common verb+noun pairs (e.g., "receive an award") quickly identify the best fit for phrases about honors or recognition.'),
  ('a505b053-733f-5f52-adac-422d9bbefdd8', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence states the data were "mostly inaccessible until a recent data transfer project made them _____ ." The word needed must mean the opposite of inaccessible; "available" precisely conveys that the data can now be accessed and used, which matches the following clause about Renee Weber using the information.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Complicated" does not contrast with "inaccessible" and would not explain why a transfer project would change researchers'' ability to use the data.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Representative" means typical or characteristic; it does not address accessibility and so does not fit the context about access resulting from a transfer project.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Predictable" relates to forecastability, not to whether the data can be accessed; it therefore fails to complete the contrast with "inaccessible."
**Faster approach:** Identify the logical contrast or result implied by the clause after "until" (here, access enabling research) and pick the answer that expresses that opposite idea—choose the word meaning "able to be accessed" ("available").'),
  ('03180e92-c554-5ba1-8a0c-b50cd8049ec7', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence describes a method for determining how important a scholar’s research is; to "assess" means to evaluate or judge that importance, which fits precisely with tracking citation frequency as an evaluation method.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Utilize" means to make use of something; the sentence is about evaluating importance, not using the importance of research.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Influence" is a noun or verb referring to having an effect on something; it does not fit grammatically or logically in the phrase "One way to influence the importance..." and conflicts with the intended meaning of measuring importance.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Undermine" means to weaken or reduce, which is the opposite of what the sentence conveys; citation tracking does not weaken a work’s importance.
**Faster approach:** Substitute each choice into the sentence and read for grammatical fit and meaning; eliminate words that change the sentence’s intended action (here, evaluation), leaving the best choice, "assess."'),
  ('93f32211-f90e-5aac-85fa-6b10ac243f9b', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage first defines the biological process of horizontal gene transfer and gives examples of organisms in which it occurs, then explains the significance of studying that process by linking understanding prevention to mitigation of dangerous pathogens. The structure is therefore description followed by explanation of importance.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage does not provide a history of research or a timeline; it gives a definition and a present-day implication, not a historical account or explicit research direction.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage does not argue that a specific organism poses a threat and then offer avoidance strategies; it discusses a genetic process that can increase resistance and why studying prevention matters.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Although two organisms are named as examples, the passage does not compare their similarities and differences; it uses them only to illustrate where the process occurs.
**Faster approach:** Read the first sentence for topic (what) and the final sentence for purpose (why) to match description + importance quickly.'),
  ('2ec2d908-5728-5496-a396-d7a8d010e12f', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** Text 2 explicitly questions whether naming a city a City of Gastronomy automatically leads to awareness or tourism: Park and colleagues argue that cities must create effective marketing strategies and warn that "without an intentional effort... many current and potential visitors... may not even be aware" of the city’s food. That directly casts doubt on Text 1’s claim that the honor is well known, especially beyond local audiences, so the author of Text 2 would most likely suggest the designation may not be well known outside immediate circles.
**Choice A is incorrect.** Text 2 does not praise UNESCO’s selection or argue it was deserved; instead it emphasizes the need for marketing to make the designation effective.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Text 2 focuses on the lack of awareness and the need for promotion rather than enumerating other benefits of the designation.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Text 2 attributes possible tourism shortfalls to insufficient marketing, not to the actions of local chefs or who earned the recognition.
**Faster approach:** For cross-text contradiction questions, spot language that disputes the highlighted claim (words like "may not even be aware" or calls for action). The choice that expresses that direct counterpoint is the correct answer.'),
  ('de424ce7-fdc3-5538-9b18-1a73aa1d9f19', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage states that Davis and Evans “omitted parts, such as the aria ‘I Got Plenty of Nuthin'',” which means the aria is in Gershwin’s original but was left out of the 1958 adaptation.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although the passage says they sometimes made “brief gestures” toward Gershwin’s melodies, it explicitly gives the aria as an example of a part that was omitted, so the text does not support even a brief quotation of that specific aria.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage gives no information about listeners’ opinions of the aria or Gershwin’s broader body of work.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage mentions the albums Quiet Nights and Milestones but does not state that Davis adapted that aria for Milestones; in fact, it says the aria was omitted from the Davis–Evans adaptation.
**Faster approach:** Look for the exact phrase naming the aria and any verb describing what was done to it (e.g., “omitted”); that single clause directly answers the question without scanning the whole passage.'),
  ('89d72ff4-0377-55f0-a28b-dbef2cbf2a58', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage contrasts a sermon (spoken words) with a printed book, saying a sermon "extends no farther than the strength of memory" while a printed book is "a record... always ready to renew its acquaintance with his memory" and "conveys its contents for ages to come." This emphasizes that printed words have greater permanence than spoken messages.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The passage says the opposite: spoken words depend on memory and do not endure as reliably as print, so people are not less likely to forget a spoken message than a printed one.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The passage does not claim that only experts should be heeded; it discusses the medium (spoken vs. printed) and its durability, not the speaker’s expertise.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Although the passage notes an author may be "forgotten in his grave," that detail supports the point about print’s longevity rather than claiming most authors won’t be remembered; it’s not the main idea.
**Faster approach:** Scan for contrast words (e.g., "and," "while," "extends no farther") to find the central comparison; choose the answer that restates that contrast (spoken = transient, printed = permanent).'),
  ('f77d67ed-179c-590a-ba23-d4fd42697e31', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The table lists approximate weights of 400, 800, 375, and 200 pounds. The smallest weight shown is 200 pounds, which corresponds to bear identification number 122, a male that was 3 years old, so the statement is best completed by "male that was 3 years old."
**Choice A is incorrect.** The female that was 13 years old has an approximate weight of 375 pounds, which is not the lowest weight in the table.
**Choice B is incorrect.** The female that was 6 years old weighs about 400 pounds, which is greater than the lowest weight (200 pounds).
**Choice C is incorrect.** The male that was 10 years old has an approximate weight of 800 pounds, the largest weight listed, not the lowest.
**Faster approach:** Scan the Approximate weight column and pick the smallest number; then read that row to identify the bear’s sex and age.'),
  ('e9e34ac3-873e-5999-8d9a-e0441717a756', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The table gives the cumulative counts of each species at the three stations. For the streaky rockskipper row, the entry under Station 3 is 610, so the sentence should read that the researchers counted 610 streaky rockskippers at station 3.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The value 90 in the table is the count for Cocos frillgoby at station 3, not the streaky rockskipper.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The value 125 is the count for streaky rockskipper at station 1, not at station 3.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The value 249 is the count for barred flagtail at station 1, not the streaky rockskipper at station 3.
**Faster approach:** Locate the species row in the table, then read across to the station column given in the sentence; directly use that intersection value. This works for any similar table-based evidence question.'),
  ('b668583e-a16d-533f-a206-eb4ee01564e6', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence explicitly links Washington, DC, to the narrator’s Southern homeland by saying “soil of my homeland falls like a fertile shower upon the lean streets of [Washington, DC],” using figurative language (a simile and imagery) to show the South physically and sensorially reaching into the urban environment. The words “soil,” “homeland,” and “fertile shower” create a direct, evocative connection between the rural South and the city streets, which most effectively supports the claim that Toomer links the narrator’s urban present and rural past.
**Choice B is incorrect.** This detail describes trees in planters on V Street and evokes urban landscaping, but it does not use figurative language to connect Washington to the rural South; it’s a literal description of city streets rather than a metaphorical linking of places.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The Capitol dome simile (“gray ghost ship drifting in from sea”) creates an image of the building and suggests distance or otherness, but it compares the dome to a ship rather than connecting the city to the narrator’s Southern homeland.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage about train whistles uses personification and metaphor (“iterated gasps and sobs,” “crude music”), but it characterizes sounds of the valley rather than explicitly linking Washington, DC, with the rural South in the way the correct quotation does.
**Faster approach:** For command-of-evidence questions asking which quotation “connects” two places or ideas, scan each choice for explicit relational language (words like “homeland,” “like,” “upon,” or direct comparisons). Prefer the option that names both elements and uses figurative wording to bridge them.'),
  ('23cf0891-139d-5b97-be1e-47a8da0bf644', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It gives a clear, quantitative statement that supports the claim: stating that roughly 70% of the world’s water withdrawals are for irrigation directly shows irrigation is humanity’s principal (largest) use of water.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It supplies historical information about the first known irrigation system but gives no evidence about how irrigation ranks among human uses of water.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It describes a modern irrigation technology used in parts of the southwestern United States but does not provide any comparative or quantitative evidence that irrigation is the principal human use of water.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It discusses the design features of an ancient irrigation system and their endurance, but it does not address the relative magnitude of irrigation’s use of water.
**Faster approach:** For a claim about what is “principal” pick the answer with a statistic or direct comparative language indicating magnitude or proportion.'),
  ('32ee32bc-902b-5d41-b71f-62a44cbe2e92', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The researcher’s hypothesis predicts that languages closer to the original homeland in Africa will retain more sounds, while those farther away will retain fewer. Choice B reports that Eastern Europe (closer to Africa) has languages with more sounds than East Asia (farther from Africa), which directly matches the predicted negative relationship between distance from Africa and number of sounds.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It says a region farther from Africa (northwestern North America) has more sounds than a region closer to Africa (East Asia), which contradicts the hypothesis.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It reports that a region closer to Africa (Western Europe) has fewer sounds than a region farther away (East Asia), which also contradicts the predicted pattern.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It compares two regions at roughly the same distance from Africa; finding a difference in sounds there would not support a distance-based gradient predicted by the hypothesis.
**Faster approach:** Translate the hypothesis into a simple rule (“closer to Africa = more sounds”). Evaluate each choice by checking which region is closer to Africa and whether it has more sounds; eliminate any choice that violates the rule.'),
  ('6109ad94-5b09-55cf-9c09-3e463fc4d960', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The passage reports that EBF repels mosquitoes by activating a single odor receptor, Or31, and that Or31 is present in all disease-carrying mosquito species; therefore, the most useful step in developing new repellents is to find molecular components like EBF that specifically target and activate Or31 across species.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Improving extraction methods for EBF does not address the broader goal of finding repellents that exploit the shared Or31 receptor across species.
**Choice C is incorrect.** Locating receptors on the antennae is a descriptive task unrelated to producing repellents that act on the universally present Or31 receptor.
**Choice D is incorrect.** Determining how many receptors a molecule can activate is not as directly useful as identifying molecules that specifically target the Or31 receptor known to provide repellency across species.
**Faster approach:** Focus on the passage’s causal claim (EBF repels by activating Or31) and the generalization (Or31 is in all disease-carrying species); choose the answer that applies that generalization to repellent development (find similar molecules).'),
  ('80e7ffee-de46-5f86-a214-098e0315e385', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The passage reports that most young people view volunteering as helping individuals but only 6% see it as fixing society overall; the natural inference is that young people may be
underestimating volunteering’s broader impact, so volunteering may be even more helpful than many young people think it is.
**Choice A is incorrect.** The passage says youths see volunteering as helping individuals, but it does not say they think volunteering is limited to their own communities; that introduces an unsupported locality claim.
**Choice C is incorrect.** The choice claims volunteering helps society more than individuals, which contradicts the survey results that show youths perceive greater individual-level benefit.
**Choice D is incorrect.** The passage provides no evidence about youths’ ability to find volunteer opportunities; it only reports beliefs about the effects of volunteering.
**Faster approach:** Notice the contrast (most cite individual help vs. only 6% cite societal impact) and pick the answer that explains the discrepancy by saying youths undervalue the broader effects of volunteering.'),
  ('d9af71ff-dafe-5562-adcd-03e82ab856fc', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The sentence requires a declarative noun clause after "why": "his voice acting work is why he has become such a celebrated talent." That clause follows normal subject-verb order ("he has become") and ends with a period to complete the statement.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It inverts subject and auxiliary into a question form ("has he become"), which is ungrammatical here because "why" introduces a statement, not a question.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although it uses the inverted question order, it ends with a period; the inversion still makes it ungrammatical as a declarative complement to "is why."
**Choice C is incorrect.** This is a direct question (ending with a question mark) and does not fit as the complement of "is why" in the main sentence.
**Faster approach:** Recognize that "why" here introduces a declarative noun clause; use normal subject-verb order and a period, not question inversion or a question mark.'),
  ('5093beb3-8e8d-5b13-9fff-b30cf78e9484', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence refers to a single historical figure in a past time period ("in the late 1800s"), so the simple past tense "was" correctly matches the subject and time frame: "Ann Greely of Maine was a fierce advocate..."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Have been" is a present perfect plural or singular with auxiliary that requires a present reference or plural subject (and would not fit the clearly past time indicator "in the late 1800s").
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Are" is present tense plural and does not agree with the singular subject "Ann Greely" nor with the past time frame.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Were" is past tense plural (or subjunctive) and does not agree with the singular subject; the correct singular past form is "was."
**Faster approach:** Match tense and number: use simple past for a single person referenced with a past time phrase, so choose "was."'),
  ('0fdeb694-6427-5d01-9d1d-9460402c81dc', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The grammatical subject is the singular noun phrase "The city of Quito," so the verb must be singular present. "Is" correctly matches the singular subject and completes the sentence: "The city of Quito in Ecuador is one of the rare places..."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "Have been" is plural and a perfect progressive form; it does not agree with the singular subject and also changes the intended simple-stative meaning.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Are" is a plural present-tense verb and does not agree with the singular subject "The city."
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Are being" is plural progressive and does not agree with the singular subject; it also implies an ongoing action, which conflicts with the static description intended.
**Faster approach:** Identify the subject ("The city of Quito") and choose the verb form that agrees in number; for a singular subject use a singular present-tense verb like "is."'),
  ('0897730d-8583-58dd-9342-7c2ed2cb4eeb', 2, null, '**Choice C is correct.** The sentence requires the paired prepositions "from" and "to" to indicate the temperature range: "from 18.4°C at 11:00 a.m. to 16.2°C at 7:00 p.m." No extra commas or colons should interrupt the "from...to" construction, so "from 18.4°C at" fits correctly after the comma already present in the passage.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It inserts an extraneous comma after "from" (", from,18.4°C at ..."), creating a punctuation error and separating the paired elements improperly.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It both places an unnecessary comma after "from" and adds a comma after "at," disrupting the required "from...to" phrase and mispunctuating the time expression.
**Choice D is incorrect.** A colon after "from" ("from:18.4°C at,") is ungrammatical here; colons do not belong inside a simple "from...to" range and the extra comma further breaks standard punctuation.
**Faster approach:** Look for a paired construction ("from...to") and choose the option that leaves that pair intact with no extra internal punctuation.'),
  ('13d5e649-5059-5521-b154-454453943cc1', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The phrase "capable of measuring" requires a noun or noun phrase to follow; "occurring" is a present participle that correctly modifies "chemical reactions," yielding "measuring chemical reactions occurring within molecules." This creates a clear, grammatically correct modifier.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Occurred" is a past-tense verb and cannot directly modify "chemical reactions" in this participial phrase; it would create an ungrammatical construction.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Occur" is the base form of the verb and does not function as a modifier here; "measuring chemical reactions occur within..." is ungrammatical.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Will occur" shifts the tense to future and also cannot serve as a modifier in this structure; it conflicts with the noun phrase that follows "measuring."
**Faster approach:** Recognize that after "measuring" you need a participial modifier for "chemical reactions," so choose the present participle form ("-ing").'),
  ('f275cac5-954b-53e9-8e66-3c2385e324da', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The phrase beginning with "which" is a nonrestrictive clause that adds extra information about the Harbor Park bicycle path, so it must be set off by commas on both sides: "...path, which is about 0.38 miles long, have become..." The surrounding commas mark the clause as parenthetical and do not change the main plural subject "paths" or the verb "have become."
**Choice A is incorrect.** It has a comma before "which" but is missing the required comma after the clause, leaving the sentence punctuation incomplete.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It omits both commas, failing to mark the clause as nonrestrictive; nonrestrictive clauses introduced by "which" require commas.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It includes a comma after the clause but omits the comma before "which," so the clause is not properly set off.
**Faster approach:** Remember that clauses beginning with "which" are nonrestrictive and must be enclosed in commas; immediately choose the option with commas on both sides of the clause.'),
  ('f675c41a-108c-5f7d-a269-727f932620bb', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The sentence describes a sequence of procedural steps (first, next, ...), so a transition that signals the final step in the sequence fits best. "Finally," clearly indicates the concluding action of removing the cloth after soaking and simmering, which matches the passage’s progression.
**Choice B is incorrect.** "However," signals contrast, which doesn’t make sense here because the sentence continues the sequence rather than introducing a contrasting idea.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "Therefore," signals logical consequence; while there is cause and effect in dyeing, the passage is listing steps, and "therefore" would awkwardly emphasize causation rather than the final step.
**Choice D is incorrect.** "Actually," introduces a correction or surprising detail, which is not needed in this straightforward procedural sequence.
**Faster approach:** For transition questions, identify whether the passage is listing steps (look for words like "first/next") or showing contrast/cause; then choose the transition that matches that rhetorical structure (sequence → "finally/then").'),
  ('2e96d9db-10e1-57ba-807f-9072b0a05647', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The passage states that English was rarely used in literature until Chaucer pioneered its literary use, and the next clause gives evidence of his pioneering role by noting that his manuscripts contain the first documented uses of many English words. "In fact," is a transition that introduces reinforcing evidence and fits the logical flow from claim to supporting detail.
**Choice A is incorrect.** "Besides," signals an additional point of similar weight but does not specifically signal the strong, illustrative evidence that follows; it is weaker than "In fact."
**Choice B is incorrect.** "However," signals contrast, which contradicts the passage’s intent to provide supporting evidence about Chaucer.
**Choice C is incorrect.** "On the contrary," also signals opposition and would conflict with the passage’s continuation presenting confirming information.
**Faster approach:** Look for whether the next clause gives supporting evidence (use "In fact" or similar) or a contrast (use "however"/"on the contrary").'),
  ('85603b04-4cd6-5b27-bfc3-6fa2f5f51822', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** It directly states that Stradivari’s violins are renowned among musicians for their sound quality, which matches the note that “Musicians prize his Stradivarius violins for their famed sound quality.” This sentence most effectively emphasizes the reason the instruments are prized.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It mentions naming conventions of existing instruments but does not explain why musicians value Stradivarius violins.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It gives Stradivari’s lifespan, a biographical detail, but nothing about the instruments’ desirability or sound quality.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It notes a specific owner (Leonora Jackson) but does not address why musicians prize the violins.
**Faster approach:** For questions asking “why” or “emphasize why,” pick the sentence that explicitly states a cause or reason—in this case, the line about famed sound quality.'),
  ('ccbc5696-70a3-542e-9d05-6967ec80244b', 0, null, '**Choice A is correct.** The notes state that Gianpaolo Bellini is an Italian particle physicist and that he is known for his research on solar and geoneutrinos; they also note that neutrinos are among the least understood subatomic particles. Choice A combines these facts into a clear, specific statement that Bellini has worked to advance scientific understanding of neutrinos through his research on solar and geoneutrinos.
**Choice B is incorrect.** Although it correctly describes neutrinos, it does not specify Bellini’s role or mention his research on solar and geoneutrinos.
**Choice C is incorrect.** This sentence gives background about neutrinos’ discovery but does not refer to Bellini or his research.
**Choice D is incorrect.** This choice is vague; it identifies Bellini’s field but fails to specify that his notable work concerns solar and geoneutrinos.
**Faster approach:** Eliminate choices that don’t mention Bellini’s research on solar and geoneutrinos; the correct answer will explicitly link Bellini to that specific area of neutrino study.'),
  ('f22517de-3bcf-5671-8fe3-099114d9804d', 1, null, '**Choice B is correct.** The sentence names both the buffalo horn spoon and the beaded leather purse and states a shared characteristic drawn directly from the notes: both are cultural artifacts featured in the Hina Hanta archive and relevant to Choctaw history. This directly emphasizes the similarity the student wants to highlight.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It mentions only the spoon and so fails to emphasize a similarity between the two artifacts.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It emphasizes a contrast in dates (difference) rather than a similarity; it does not highlight what the two items have in common.
**Choice D is incorrect.** It focuses on relative recency (a difference) instead of stating a shared attribute of the two artifacts.
**Faster approach:** For synthesis tasks, pick the choice that explicitly mentions both items and states a shared feature; eliminate options that mention only one item or that emphasize differences in date or status.'),
  ('353d5449-ae7e-590a-b018-63d71de85f23', 3, null, '**Choice D is correct.** The notes state that "Color consultants create trend reports on the popular colors at a given Fashion Week," so D most directly and effectively explains the consultants'' role by saying that each Fashion Week color consultants compile reports on shades popular with designers.
**Choice A is incorrect.** It restates a detail about a purple shade from 2018 but does not explain the general role of color consultants in creating trend reports.
**Choice B is incorrect.** It highlights a 2019 report about deep red but, like A, only gives a specific example rather than explaining the consultants’ overall function.
**Choice C is incorrect.** It summarizes which shades were favored by designers in 2018 and 2019 but does not state that color consultants compile reports about those shades.
**Faster approach:** Look for the sentence that generalizes the role (creates trend reports); pick the choice that paraphrases that general statement rather than one that repeats a specific example.')
on conflict (question_id) do update set correct_index = excluded.correct_index, correct_answers = excluded.correct_answers,
  explanation = excluded.explanation;
